text:
Another two spaces that we can think of as ``the same'' are 
\( \mathcal{P}_2 \), the space of quadratic polynomials, and \( \mathbb{R}^3 \). 
A natural correspondence is this.
\begin{equation*}
 a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2
 \quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
 \begin{pmatrix}a_0 \\ a_1 \\ a_2\end{pmatrix}
 \tag*{\mbox{(e.g., $1+2x+3x^2\,\longleftrightarrow\,\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}$)}}
\end{equation*}
This preserves structure: corresponding elements add in a corresponding way
\begin{equation*}
 \mbox{\begin{tabular}{r}
 \( a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2 \) \\
 +\,\,\( b_0+b_1x+b_2x^2 \) \\ \hline
 \( (a_0+b_0)+(a_1+b_1)x+(a_2+b_2)x^2 \)
 \end{tabular} }
 \longleftrightarrow
 \begin{pmatrix}a_0 \\ a_1 \\ a_2\end{pmatrix}
 +\begin{pmatrix}b_0 \\ b_1 \\ b_2\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}a_0+b_0 \\ a_1+b_1 \\ a_2+b_2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
and scalar multiplication corresponds also.
\begin{equation*}
 r\cdot(a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2)=
 (ra_0)+(ra_1)x+(ra_2)x^2
 \quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
 r\cdot\begin{pmatrix}a_0 \\ a_1 \\ a_2\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}ra_0 \\ ra_1 \\ ra_2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

