text:
If \( \map{h}{\mathbb{R}^3}{\mathcal{P}_1} \) is
\begin{equation*}
 \begin{pmatrix}a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3\end{pmatrix}
 \mapsunder{h}
 (2a_1+a_2)+(-a_3)x
\end{equation*}
then where
\begin{equation*}
 B=
 \sequence{\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix},
 \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix},
 \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} }
 \qquad
 D=
 \langle 1+x,-1+x\rangle
\end{equation*}
the action of \( h \) on \( B \) is this.
\begin{equation*}
 \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\mapsunder{h}-x
 \qquad \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\mapsunder{h}2
 \qquad \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\mapsunder{h}4
\end{equation*}
A simple calculation
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{-x}{D}=\begin{pmatrix}-1/2 \\ -1/2\end{pmatrix}_D
 \quad \rep{2}{D}=\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}_D 
 \quad \rep{4}{D}=\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ -2\end{pmatrix}_D 
\end{equation*}
shows that this is the matrix representing $h$ with respect to the bases.
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{h}{B,D}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}
 -1/2 &1 &2 \\
 -1/2 &-1 &-2
 \end{pmatrix}_{B,D}
\end{equation*}

