text:
We can do a similar exploration for the sum of two maps.
Suppose that two linear maps with the same domain and codomain 
\( \map{f,g}{\mathbb{R}^2}{\mathbb{R}^2} \) are represented 
with respect to bases $B$ and $D$ by these matrices.
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{f}{B,D}=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 1 &3 \\
 2 &0 
 \end{pmatrix}
 \qquad
 \rep{g}{B,D}=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 -2 &-1 \\
 2 &4
 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Recall the definition of sum: if
$f$ does $\vec{v}\mapsto\vec{u}$ and 
$g$ does $\vec{v}\mapsto\vec{w}$ then
$f+g$ is the function whose action is $\vec{v}\mapsto\vec{u}+\vec{w}$.
Let these be the representations of the input and output vectors. 
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{\vec{v}}{B}=\begin{pmatrix}v_1 \\ v_2\end{pmatrix}
 \qquad
 \rep{\vec{u}}{D}=\begin{pmatrix}u_1 \\ u_2\end{pmatrix}
 \quad
 \rep{\vec{w}}{D}=\begin{pmatrix}w_1 \\ w_2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Where $D=\sequence{\vec{\delta}_1,\vec{\delta}_2}$ we have
$\vec{u}+\vec{w}=(u_1\vec{\delta}_1+u_2\vec{\delta}_2)
 +(w_1\vec{\delta}_1+w_2\vec{\delta}_2)
 =(u_1+w_1)\vec{\delta}_1+(u_2+w_2)\vec{\delta}_2$
and so this is the representation of the vector sum.
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{\vec{u}+\vec{w}}{D}=\begin{pmatrix}u_1+w_1 \\ u_2+w_2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Thus, since these represent the actions of of the maps $f$ and $g$ on the 
input $\vec{v}$
\begin{equation*}
 \begin{pmatrix}
 1 &3 \\
 2 &0 
 \end{pmatrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}v_1 \\ v_2\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}v_1+3v_2 \\ 2v_1\end{pmatrix}
 \qquad
 \begin{pmatrix}
 -2 &-1 \\
 2 &4
 \end{pmatrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}v_1 \\ v_2\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}-2v_1-v_2 \\ 2v_1+4v_2\end{pmatrix} 
\end{equation*}
adding the entries represents the action of the map $f+g$.
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{f+g}{B,D}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}v_1 \\ v_2\end{pmatrix}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}-v_1+2v_2 \\ 4v_1+4v_2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Therefore, we 
compute the matrix representing the function sum by adding the entries of 
the matrices representing the functions.
\begin{equation*} 
 \rep{f+g}{B,D}=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 -1 &2 \\
 4 &4 
 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

question:
We can do a similar exploration for the sum of two maps.
Suppose that two linear maps with the same domain and codomain 
\( \map{f,g}{\mathbb{R}^2}{\mathbb{R}^2} \) are represented 
with respect to bases $B$ and $D$ by these matrices.
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{f}{B,D}=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 1 &3 \\
 2 &0 
 \end{pmatrix}
 \qquad
 \rep{g}{B,D}=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 -2 &-1 \\
 2 &4
 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Recall the definition of sum: if
$f$ does $\vec{v}\mapsto\vec{u}$ and 
$g$ does $\vec{v}\mapsto\vec{w}$ then
$f+g$ is the function whose action is $\vec{v}\mapsto\vec{u}+\vec{w}$.
Let these be the representations of the input and output vectors. 
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{\vec{v}}{B}=\begin{pmatrix}v_1 \\ v_2\end{pmatrix}
 \qquad
 \rep{\vec{u}}{D}=\begin{pmatrix}u_1 \\ u_2\end{pmatrix}
 \quad
 \rep{\vec{w}}{D}=\begin{pmatrix}w_1 \\ w_2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Where $D=\sequence{\vec{\delta}_1,\vec{\delta}_2}$ we have
$\vec{u}+\vec{w}=(u_1\vec{\delta}_1+u_2\vec{\delta}_2)
 +(w_1\vec{\delta}_1+w_2\vec{\delta}_2)
 =(u_1+w_1)\vec{\delta}_1+(u_2+w_2)\vec{\delta}_2$
and so this is the representation of the vector sum.
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{\vec{u}+\vec{w}}{D}=\begin{pmatrix}u_1+w_1 \\ u_2+w_2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Thus, since these represent the actions of of the maps $f$ and $g$ on the 
input $\vec{v}$
\begin{equation*}
 \begin{pmatrix}
 1 &3 \\
 2 &0 
 \end{pmatrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}v_1 \\ v_2\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}v_1+3v_2 \\ 2v_1\end{pmatrix}
 \qquad
 \begin{pmatrix}
 -2 &-1 \\
 2 &4
 \end{pmatrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}v_1 \\ v_2\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}-2v_1-v_2 \\ 2v_1+4v_2\end{pmatrix} 
\end{equation*}
adding the entries represents the action of the map $f+g$.
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{f+g}{B,D}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}v_1 \\ v_2\end{pmatrix}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}-v_1+2v_2 \\ 4v_1+4v_2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Therefore, we 
compute the matrix representing the function sum by adding the entries of 
the matrices representing the functions.
\begin{equation*} 
 \rep{f+g}{B,D}=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 -1 &2 \\
 4 &4 
 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

text:
To convert from the representation of a vector $\vec{v}$ 
with respect to \( B \) 
to its representation with respect to
\( D \) use the change of basis matrix.
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{\identity}{B,D}\,\rep{\vec{v}}{B}=\rep{\vec{v}}{D}
\end{equation*}
Conversely, if left-multiplication by a matrix changes bases 
$M\cdot\rep{\vec{v}}{B}=\rep{\vec{v}}{D}$
then $M$ is a change of basis matrix.

