text:
This is a basis for \( \mathbb{R}^2 \).
\begin{equation*}
 \sequence{ \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 4\end{pmatrix},\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} }
\end{equation*}
It is linearly independent
\begin{equation*}
 c_1\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}
 \quad\implies\quad
 \begin{linsys}{2}
 2c_1 &+ &1c_2 &= &0 \\
 4c_1 &+ &1c_2 &= &0 
 \end{linsys}
 \quad\implies\quad
 c_1=c_2=0
\end{equation*}
and it spans \( \mathbb{R}^2 \).
\begin{equation*}
 \begin{linsys}{2}
 2c_1 &+ &1c_2 &= &x \\
 4c_1 &+ &1c_2 &= &y 
 \end{linsys}
 \quad\implies\quad
 c_2=2x-y\text{\ and\ } c_1=(y-x)/2
\end{equation*}

