text:
The set \( \matspace_{\nbym{2}{2}} \) of \( \nbym{2}{2} \) matrices with
real number entries is a vector space under the natural 
entry-by-entry operations.
\begin{equation*}
 \begin{pmatrix}
 a &b \\
 c &d
 \end{pmatrix}
 +
 \begin{pmatrix}
 w &x \\
 y &z
 \end{pmatrix}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}
 a+w &b+x \\
 c+y &d+z
 \end{pmatrix}
 \qquad
 r\cdot
 \begin{pmatrix}
 a &b \\
 c &d
 \end{pmatrix}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}
 ra &rb \\
 rc &rd
 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
As in the prior example, we can think of this space as
``the same'' as \( \mathbb{R}^4 \).

