\begin{equation*}
 B=\sequence{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}}
 \qquad
 D=\langle x^2, x^2+x, x^2+x+1\rangle
\end{equation*}
Then let $\map{h}{\mathbb{R}^2}{\mathcal{P}_2}$ be the function defined by $H$.
We will compute the image under $h$ of this member of the domain.
\begin{equation*}
 \vec{v}=\begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
The computation is straightforward.
\begin{equation*}
 \rep{h(\vec{v})}{D}
 =H\cdot\rep{\vec{v}}{B}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}
 1 &2 \\
 3 &4 \\
 5 &6
 \end{pmatrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}-1/2 \\ -5/2\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}-11/2 \\ -23/2 \\ -35/2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
From its representation, computation of $h(\vec{v})$ is routine
$(-11/2)(x^2)-(23/2)(x^2+x)-(35/2)(x^2+x+1)
=(-69/2)x^2-(58/2)x-(35/2)$.

