The \textbf{matrix-multiplicative product}
of the \( \nbym{m}{r} \)
matrix \( G \) and the \( \nbym{r}{n} \) matrix \( H \) is the
\( \nbym{m}{n} \) matrix \( P \), where
\begin{equation*}
 p_{i,j}
 =
 g_{i,1}h_{1,j}+g_{i,2}h_{2,j}+\dots+g_{i,r}h_{r,j}
\end{equation*}
so that
the \( i,j \)-th entry of the product is the dot product of the
\( i \)-th row of the first matrix with the \( j \)-th column of the second.
\begin{equation*}
 GH=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 &\vdots \\
 g_{i,1} &g_{i,2} &\cdots &g_{i,r} \\
 &\vdots
 \end{pmatrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}
 &h_{1,j} \\
 \cdots &h_{2,j} &\cdots \\
 &\vdots \\
 &h_{r,j}
 \end{pmatrix}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}
 &\vdots \\
 \cdots &p_{i,j} &\cdots \\
 &\vdots
 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

