text:
To produce from this basis for $\mathbb{R}^3$
\begin{equation*}
 B=\sequence{
 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix},
 \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix},
 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}
 }
\end{equation*}
an orthogonal basis, start by taking the first vector unchanged.
\begin{equation*}
 \vec{\kappa}_1=\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Get \( \vec{\kappa}_2 \) by subtracting from
$\vec{\beta}_2$ 
its part in the direction of \( \vec{\kappa}_1 \).
\begin{equation*}
 \vec{\kappa}_2=\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}
 -\proj{\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}}{\spanof{\vec{\kappa}_1}}
 =\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}-\begin{pmatrix}2/3 \\ 2/3 \\ 2/3\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}-2/3 \\ 4/3 \\ -2/3\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
Find $\vec{\kappa}_3$ 
by subtracting from $\vec{\beta}_3$ the part 
in the direction of \( \vec{\kappa}_1 \) and also the part 
in the direction of \( \vec{\kappa}_2 \).
\begin{equation*}
 \vec{\kappa}_3=\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}
 -\proj{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}}{\spanof{\vec{\kappa}_1}}
 -\proj{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}}{\spanof{\vec{\kappa}_2}}
 =\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
As above, the corollary gives that the result is a basis for $\mathbb{R}^3$.
\begin{equation*}
 \sequence{
 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix},
 \begin{pmatrix}-2/3 \\ 4/3 \\ -2/3\end{pmatrix},
 \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}
 }
\end{equation*}

