text:
Even if the domain and codomain are the same, the map that the 
matrix represents depends on the 
bases that we choose.
If
\begin{equation*}
 H=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 1 &0 \\
 0 &0
 \end{pmatrix},
 \quad
 B_1=D_1=\sequence{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix},\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} },
 \quad\text{and}\quad
 B_2=D_2=\sequence{\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix},\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} },
\end{equation*}
then \( \map{h_1}{\mathbb{R}^2}{\mathbb{R}^2} \) represented by \( H \)
with respect to \( B_1,D_1 \) maps
\begin{equation*}
 \begin{pmatrix}c_1 \\ c_2\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}c_1 \\ c_2\end{pmatrix}_{B_1}
 \quad
 \mapsto
 \quad
 \begin{pmatrix}c_1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}_{D_1}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}c_1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
while \( \map{h_2}{\mathbb{R}^2}{\mathbb{R}^2} \) represented by \( H \)
with respect to \( B_2,D_2 \) is this map.
\begin{equation*}
 \begin{pmatrix}c_1 \\ c_2\end{pmatrix}
 =\begin{pmatrix}c_2 \\ c_1\end{pmatrix}_{B_2}
 \quad
 \mapsto
 \quad
 \begin{pmatrix}c_2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}_{D_2}
 =
 \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ c_2\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}
These are different functions.
The first is projection onto the \( x \)-axis while the second 
is projection onto the $y$-axis.

