text:
Parametrization finds bases for other vector spaces, not just
for solution sets of homogeneous systems.
To find a basis for this subspace of $\matspace_{2\times 2}$
\begin{equation*}
 \set{\begin{pmatrix}
 a &b \\
 c &0
 \end{pmatrix} \suchthat a+b-2c=0}
\end{equation*}
we rewrite the condition as $a=-b+2c$.
\begin{equation*}
 \set{\begin{pmatrix}
 -b+2c &b \\
 c &0
 \end{pmatrix} \suchthat b,c \in \mathbb{R}}
 =\set{b\begin{pmatrix}
 -1 &1 \\
 0 &0
 \end{pmatrix}+
 c\begin{pmatrix}
 2 &0 \\
 1 &0
 \end{pmatrix} \suchthat b,c \in \mathbb{R}}
\end{equation*}
Thus, this is a natural candidate for a basis.
\begin{equation*}
 \sequence{\begin{pmatrix}
 -1 &1 \\
 0 &0
 \end{pmatrix},
 \begin{pmatrix}
 2 &0 \\
 1 &0
 \end{pmatrix} }
\end{equation*}
The above work shows that it spans the space.
Linear independence is also easy.

